WEBVTT
NOTE how-do-scientists-know-so-much - timed from measured narration audio

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.816
Five percent.

00:00:00.816 --> 00:00:05.709
That is the fraction of the ocean floor
mapped at high resolution according to

00:00:05.709 --> 00:00:07.152
NOAA Ocean Exploration.

00:00:07.152 --> 00:00:12.882
Yet textbooks describe pressure gradients,
salinity profiles, and thermocline depths

00:00:12.882 --> 00:00:13.973
with confidence.

00:00:13.973 --> 00:00:18.281
The apparent contradiction dissolves when
you separate two questions: how much have

00:00:18.281 --> 00:00:20.720
we photographed, and how much have we
measured.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:25.452
How do scientists know so much about the
deep ocean when we've explored so little

00:00:25.452 --> 00:00:25.803
of it?

00:00:25.803 --> 00:00:31.095
High-resolution mapping means resolving
features smaller than one hundred meters

00:00:31.095 --> 00:00:31.558
across.

00:00:31.558 --> 00:00:36.254
It requires a ship to pass directly
overhead with multibeam sonar or an

00:00:36.254 --> 00:00:38.768
autonomous vehicle to survey the area.

00:00:38.768 --> 00:00:43.190
NOAA Ocean Exploration defines this
standard, and by that measure, five

00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:44.373
percent is correct.

00:00:44.373 --> 00:00:49.107
But the ocean has been measured in other
ways for more than a century.

00:00:49.107 --> 00:00:53.862
Satellite altimetry detects gravitational
anomalies caused by underwater mountains

00:00:53.862 --> 00:00:57.400
and trenches, revealing their locations
without imaging them.

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:00.819
Sonar transects sample depth along ship
tracks.

00:01:00.819 --> 00:01:03.656
Sediment cores pull up layered history.

00:01:03.656 --> 00:01:08.311
Conductivity-temperature-depth casts
measure the water column at thousands of

00:01:08.311 --> 00:01:11.213
points. Each method answers different
questions.

00:01:11.213 --> 00:01:15.945
The General Bathymetric Chart of the
Oceans, maintained by the International

00:01:15.945 --> 00:01:19.992
Hydrographic Organization and the
Intergovernmental Oceanographic

00:01:19.992 --> 00:01:23.603
Commission, compiles these measurements
into a global map.

00:01:23.603 --> 00:01:28.352
It is not high-resolution everywhere, but
it is not five percent coverage either.

00:01:28.352 --> 00:01:32.632
The distinction matters because "explored"
conflates seeing with knowing.

00:01:32.632 --> 00:01:36.594
Pressure increases by one atmosphere for
every ten meters of depth.

00:01:36.594 --> 00:01:41.265
That relationship holds in the Mariana
Trench and in the Puerto Rico Trench and

00:01:41.265 --> 00:01:43.453
in every trench not yet photographed.

00:01:43.453 --> 00:01:46.947
You do not need to visit each location to
know the pressure there.

00:01:46.947 --> 00:01:52.136
You need the depth, which sonar provides,
and the equation, which physics provides.

00:01:52.136 --> 00:01:55.387
The same applies to temperature and
salinity.

00:01:55.387 --> 00:02:00.445
Conductivity-temperature-depth
instruments, or CTDs, lower through the

00:02:00.445 --> 00:02:02.829
water column and record profiles.

00:02:02.829 --> 00:02:06.670
Thousands of these casts, taken over
decades, reveal patterns.

00:02:06.670 --> 00:02:10.820
The thermocline, the layer where
temperature drops sharply, sits at

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:13.608
predictable depths in different ocean
basins.

00:02:13.608 --> 00:02:18.709
Salinity varies with evaporation and river
input, but those inputs are measurable.

00:02:18.709 --> 00:02:23.312
Models interpolate between sample points
using known circulation patterns.

00:02:23.312 --> 00:02:25.125
Interpolation is not guessing.

00:02:25.125 --> 00:02:28.509
It is estimating between measurements
using constraints.

00:02:28.509 --> 00:02:33.404
If you measure temperature at one hundred
meters and at three hundred meters, and

00:02:33.404 --> 00:02:38.058
you know the water is stratified, you can
estimate the gradient between them.

00:02:38.058 --> 00:02:41.744
The estimate carries uncertainty, but it
is not a blind spot.

00:02:41.744 --> 00:02:45.555
Oceanographers do not measure every cubic
meter of seawater.

00:02:45.555 --> 00:02:50.891
They measure representative samples and
test whether patterns hold across

00:02:50.891 --> 00:02:55.570
The oxygen minimum zone, a layer where
dissolved oxygen drops to near zero,

00:02:55.570 --> 00:03:00.187
appears in all major oceans at depths
between two hundred and one thousand

00:03:00.187 --> 00:03:00.624
meters.

00:03:00.624 --> 00:03:03.805
It was not discovered by mapping every
ocean.

00:03:03.805 --> 00:03:08.895
It was discovered by measuring oxygen at
depth in multiple locations and

00:03:08.895 --> 00:03:10.592
recognizing the pattern.

00:03:10.592 --> 00:03:14.875
Once the pattern is established, you can
predict where else it occurs.

00:03:14.875 --> 00:03:18.131
Predictions are then tested with new
measurements.

00:03:18.131 --> 00:03:21.062
This is how scientific knowledge
accumulates.

00:03:21.062 --> 00:03:25.035
You do not need exhaustive coverage to
identify a regularity.

00:03:25.035 --> 00:03:29.812
The Argo program, a collaboration among
dozens of nations, maintains a fleet of

00:03:29.812 --> 00:03:34.529
nearly four thousand autonomous floats
drifting through the upper two thousand

00:03:34.529 --> 00:03:35.739
meters of the ocean.

00:03:35.739 --> 00:03:40.608
Each float surfaces every ten days,
transmits its data, and dives again.

00:03:40.608 --> 00:03:45.117
NOAA and the Argo Steering Team report
that these floats provide temperature and

00:03:45.117 --> 00:03:48.836
salinity profiles with a spatial
resolution of about three hundred

00:03:48.836 --> 00:03:49.456
kilometers.

00:03:49.456 --> 00:03:54.551
That is not high-resolution imaging, but
it is systematic, repeated measurement of

00:03:54.551 --> 00:03:57.099
the water column across all ocean basins.

00:03:57.099 --> 00:04:01.846
The data reveal seasonal changes, current
shifts, and heat distribution.

00:04:01.846 --> 00:04:05.275
They feed into climate models and weather
forecasts.

00:04:05.275 --> 00:04:10.180
The floats do not photograph the seafloor,
but they measure the water above it

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:10.997
continuously.

00:04:10.997 --> 00:04:15.689
The Atlantic holds roughly eight hundred
floats, the Pacific about nineteen

00:04:15.689 --> 00:04:19.942
hundred, the Indian Ocean around six
hundred, and the Southern Ocean

00:04:19.942 --> 00:04:25.009
approximately four hundred, though these
numbers shift as floats are deployed and

00:04:25.009 --> 00:04:25.509
retired.

00:04:25.509 --> 00:04:31.441
'Scientists say' is almost meaningless on
its own. Which scientists? How many?

00:04:31.441 --> 00:04:34.179
Was it one paper? Was it replicated?

00:04:34.179 --> 00:04:38.536
Multibeam sonar emits sound waves in a fan
shape and times their return.

00:04:38.536 --> 00:04:43.039
The result is a swath of depth
measurements, tens of kilometers wide,

00:04:43.039 --> 00:04:44.475
along the ship's path.

00:04:44.475 --> 00:04:48.473
A single research cruise can map thousands
of square kilometers.

00:04:48.473 --> 00:04:51.659
The technology does not require a human to
descend.

00:04:51.659 --> 00:04:53.715
It does not require a camera.

00:04:53.715 --> 00:04:59.389
It measures distance by timing sound, and
sound travels predictably in seawater.

00:04:59.389 --> 00:05:04.379
The speed of sound in seawater varies with
temperature, salinity, and pressure, but

00:05:04.379 --> 00:05:08.227
those variables are measurable, so the
variation is correctable.

00:05:08.227 --> 00:05:10.523
Satellite altimetry works differently.

00:05:10.523 --> 00:05:13.725
It measures the height of the sea surface
from orbit.

00:05:13.725 --> 00:05:18.033
Underwater mountains pull water toward
them slightly, creating a bulge.

00:05:18.033 --> 00:05:19.792
Trenches create a depression.

00:05:19.792 --> 00:05:23.809
The satellite detects these
millimeter-scale variations, and from

00:05:23.809 --> 00:05:26.837
them, scientists infer the shape of the
seafloor.

00:05:26.837 --> 00:05:31.344
The resolution is coarse, around five
kilometers, but the coverage is global.

00:05:31.344 --> 00:05:35.660
CryoSat-2 and Jason-3, both satellite
missions, provide this data.

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:41.088
It is not exploration in the sense of
sending a submersible, but it is

00:05:41.088 --> 00:05:45.914
Sediment accumulates on the ocean floor in
layers, typically a few centimeters per

00:05:45.914 --> 00:05:47.739
thousand years in the deep sea.

00:05:47.739 --> 00:05:53.332
A piston corer drives a tube into the
sediment and pulls up a column, sometimes

00:05:53.332 --> 00:05:54.677
thirty meters long.

00:05:54.677 --> 00:05:56.491
The layers preserve a record.

00:05:56.491 --> 00:06:00.368
Microfossils in the sediment indicate past
ocean temperatures.

00:06:00.368 --> 00:06:02.746
Ash layers mark volcanic eruptions.

00:06:02.746 --> 00:06:06.891
Magnetic minerals record reversals of
Earth's magnetic field.

00:06:06.891 --> 00:06:11.778
The International Ocean Discovery Program,
or IODP, has drilled more than fifteen

00:06:11.778 --> 00:06:16.666
hundred sites since nineteen sixty-eight,
reaching as deep as twenty-five hundred

00:06:16.666 --> 00:06:18.235
meters below the seafloor.

00:06:18.235 --> 00:06:19.806
Each core is a timeline.

00:06:19.806 --> 00:06:24.192
You do not need to observe the past
directly to know what happened.

00:06:24.192 --> 00:06:27.712
You need a record that preserves it, and
sediment does that.

00:06:27.712 --> 00:06:31.936
The program began with the Glomar
Challenger in nineteen sixty-eight and

00:06:31.936 --> 00:06:34.693
continues today with multiple drilling
vessels.

00:06:34.693 --> 00:06:39.078
The piston corer itself was developed in
nineteen forty-seven and revolutionized

00:06:39.078 --> 00:06:43.408
marine geology by allowing scientists to
sample deeper into the sediment column

00:06:43.408 --> 00:06:45.600
than earlier gravity corers could reach.

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:51.421
Oceanographic models simulate currents,
temperature, and salinity using equations

00:06:51.421 --> 00:06:53.936
of fluid motion and thermodynamics.

00:06:53.936 --> 00:06:57.679
They are not assumptions. They are tested
against observations.

00:06:57.679 --> 00:07:02.550
If a model predicts a current speed and a
drifter buoy measures a different speed,

00:07:02.550 --> 00:07:03.797
the model is revised.

00:07:03.797 --> 00:07:05.385
The process is iterative.

00:07:05.385 --> 00:07:10.022
Early models of the thermohaline
circulation, the global conveyor belt of

00:07:10.022 --> 00:07:11.483
deep water, were rough.

00:07:11.483 --> 00:07:13.796
Decades of measurement refined them.

00:07:13.796 --> 00:07:18.357
Today's models reproduce observed
temperature distributions and predict

00:07:18.357 --> 00:07:21.120
seasonal changes that match satellite
data.

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:25.803
They still carry uncertainty, especially
at small scales, but they are constrained

00:07:25.803 --> 00:07:27.003
by real measurements.

00:07:27.003 --> 00:07:32.213
A model that contradicts data is discarded
or corrected. That is the test.

00:07:32.213 --> 00:07:35.065
Species counts in the deep ocean are
uncertain.

00:07:35.065 --> 00:07:40.102
Estimates range from five hundred thousand
to ten million marine species, with many

00:07:40.102 --> 00:07:41.861
undiscovered in the deep sea.

00:07:41.861 --> 00:07:46.314
Ocean Census and the Census of Marine Life
report a midpoint estimate near seven

00:07:46.314 --> 00:07:50.432
hundred fifty thousand, but they are
extrapolations from limited sampling.

00:07:50.432 --> 00:07:55.537
Each trawl or submersible dive finds new
species, suggesting the true number is

00:07:55.537 --> 00:07:59.867
high, but the rate of discovery does not
yet allow a precise total.

00:07:59.867 --> 00:08:05.365
This is a real gap. The deep ocean is
undersampled for biodiversity.

00:08:05.365 --> 00:08:08.019
Scientists state the uncertainty openly.

00:08:08.019 --> 00:08:11.536
The range is wide because the evidence is
incomplete.

00:08:11.536 --> 00:08:15.802
Geothermal heat flux through the seafloor
is another example.

00:08:15.802 --> 00:08:20.907
Measurements exist at a few hundred
points, mostly near mid-ocean ridges.

00:08:20.907 --> 00:08:25.034
The global average is estimated, but local
variation is poorly known.

00:08:25.034 --> 00:08:28.144
Some areas may have higher flux than
models predict.

00:08:28.144 --> 00:08:31.493
The evidence is not sufficient to resolve
it everywhere.

00:08:31.493 --> 00:08:34.762
Knowledge is not the same as exhaustive
documentation.

00:08:34.762 --> 00:08:39.664
You do not need to photograph every square
meter to know how pressure scales with

00:08:39.664 --> 00:08:43.659
depth, or where the thermocline sits, or
how sediment layers form.

00:08:43.659 --> 00:08:48.091
You need measurements that reveal the
pattern and tests that confirm it holds

00:08:48.091 --> 00:08:48.667
elsewhere.

00:08:48.667 --> 00:08:53.292
The five percent figure is real, but it
measures one kind of data collection, not

00:08:53.292 --> 00:08:54.720
the sum of what is known.

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:59.606
The confusion arises when exploration is
treated as synonymous with understanding,

00:08:59.606 --> 00:09:04.015
when in fact most scientific knowledge
comes from instruments that sample,

00:09:04.015 --> 00:09:06.875
measure, and infer rather than observe
directly.

00:09:06.875 --> 00:09:11.637
High-resolution maps do not exist for
ninety-five percent of the ocean floor.

00:09:11.637 --> 00:09:16.003
Biodiversity in the deep sea is
undersampled, and species counts remain

00:09:16.003 --> 00:09:16.987
rough estimates.

00:09:16.987 --> 00:09:21.790
Local variation in geothermal flux,
small-scale topography, and rare

00:09:21.790 --> 00:09:25.675
geological features may not appear in
current datasets.

00:09:25.675 --> 00:09:30.297
The knowledge scientists have is robust
for large-scale patterns and physical

00:09:30.297 --> 00:09:32.037
laws, but it is not complete.

00:09:32.037 --> 00:09:37.973
Stating that limit is not a failure. It is
an accurate description of the evidence.

00:09:37.973 --> 00:09:42.459
The deep ocean is not fully explored, but
it is not unknown.

00:09:42.459 --> 00:09:47.689
Systematic measurement, physical laws, and
tested models provide knowledge that does

00:09:47.689 --> 00:09:49.931
not require visiting every location.

00:09:49.931 --> 00:09:54.085
The distinction between imaging and
understanding is the answer to the
